<section xml:id="sec-arc-length">
  <title>Arc Length of a Curve</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When an object moves along a curve, we often want to know the total
      distance it travels. In this section we build the arc-length integral,
      first in the plane and then in space, and we apply it to two motions:
      a point sweeping out a quarter circle and a paraglider spiralling to
      the ground. The key idea throughout is simple:
      <em>distance travelled is speed accumulated over time</em>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arc-length-2d">
    <title>Arc Length in 2D</title>

    <p>
      Imagine that at time <m>t=a</m> an object starts moving from an initial
      point <m>A</m> and at time <m>t=b</m> it reaches the final point
      <m>B</m>. During this period the object traces out a smooth curve
      <m>C</m> in the plane, as shown in <xref ref="fig-arc-length-curve"/>.
      How can we calculate the distance the object has travelled?
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-arc-length-curve">
      <caption>
        A smooth curve <m>C</m> traced by a moving object, from the starting
        point <m>A</m> at time <m>t=a</m> to the ending point <m>B</m> at
        time <m>t=b</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-arc-length-curve" width="55%">
        <description>
          A curve labelled C rises from a red point marked t equals a at the
          lower right to a red point marked t equals b at the upper left, with
          an arrow indicating the direction of increasing time.
        </description>
        <prefigure label="arc-length-curve" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
            <definition>C(t)=(3*cos(t), 3*sin(t))</definition>
            <definition>A=C(0.42)</definition>
            <definition>B=C(1.52)</definition>

            <coordinates bbox="[-0.5,-0.5,3.6,3.6]">
              <group at="curve-C">
                <parametric-curve function="C" domain="(0.42,1.52)" stroke="blue"
                                  thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="1.0"/>
                <label anchor="C(1.05)" alignment="ne" offset="(12,12)">
                  <m>C</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="point-A">
                <point p="A" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="A" alignment="se" offset="(6,-4)" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=a</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="point-B">
                <point p="B" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="B" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,4)" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=b</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="A smooth curve C traced by a moving object from the starting point A at time t equals a to the ending point B at time t equals b">
                <annotation ref="curve-C"
                            text="The curve C, traversed in the direction of increasing time from A to B"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="point-A" text="The starting point A, reached at time t equals a"/>
                <annotation ref="point-B" text="The ending point B, reached at time t equals b"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The idea is to measure the curve one tiny piece at a time. Over a very
      short time interval the object moves a small amount <m>dx</m>
      horizontally and <m>dy</m> vertically, so by the Pythagorean theorem it
      covers a tiny arc of length
      <me>ds=\sqrt{dx^2+dy^2}.</me>
      Adding up all these tiny lengths from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m><mdash/>that
      is, integrating<mdash/>gives the total length of the curve:
    </p>

    <me>
      \int_A^B ds = \int_A^B \sqrt{dx^2+dy^2}.
    </me>

    <p>
      To turn this into a formula we can compute, suppose the curve <m>C</m>
      is parametrized as <m>(x(t),y(t))</m> for <m>a\le t\le b</m>, or
      equivalently in vector form
      <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=x(t)\,\mathbf{i}+y(t)\,\mathbf{j}</m>. Factoring
      <m>dt</m> out of the square root converts the integral into an ordinary
      integral over time:
    </p>

    <me>
      \int_A^B ds
        = \int_a^b \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2
                       + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2}\,dt
        = \int_a^b \left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|\,dt,
    </me>

    <p>
      where in the last step we recognized the velocity vector
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf{v}(t)=\frac{d\mathbf{r}(t)}{dt}
        =\left(\frac{dx(t)}{dt},\frac{dy(t)}{dt}\right),
    </me>

    <p>
      whose length is the object's speed:
    </p>

    <me>
      \left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|
        =\sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2
             + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2}.
    </me>

    <p>
      In words: <em>arc length is the integral of speed over time</em>. This
      is the natural generalization of the familiar rule
      <q>distance equals speed times time</q> to motion whose speed and
      direction change from moment to moment.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE 1: quarter circle. Statement only; solution is a separate  -->
  <!-- <solution> environment placed after the <statement>.               -->
  <example xml:id="ex-quarter-circle">
    <title>Length of a quarter circle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the arc-length formula in 2D, calculate the length of a
        quarter circle of radius one. (We already know the answer: a full
        unit circle has circumference <m>2\pi</m>, so a quarter of it has
        length <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>. The point of this example is to see
        the formula reproduce it.)
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We can parametrize the quarter circle as
        <me>\mathbf{r}(t)=(\cos t,\sin t),\qquad 0\le t\le\frac{\pi}{2}.</me>
        Differentiating, the velocity vector and its length are
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\mathbf{v}(t) \amp = (-\sin t,\ \cos t),</mrow>
        <mrow>\left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right| \amp
              = \sqrt{\sin^2 t+\cos^2 t}=1.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        The speed is constant and equal to <m>1</m>, so the arc length is
        simply the elapsed time:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_C ds = \int_0^{\pi/2}\left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|\,dt
                  = \int_0^{\pi/2} 1\,dt = \frac{\pi}{2},
      </me>
      <p>
        as expected. Notice a useful by-product: because the speed is
        <m>1</m>, the arc length swept out up to time <m>t</m> is
        <m>s(t)=t</m> exactly. The motion is shown in
        <xref ref="fig-quarter-circle-video"/>, the position and velocity
        vectors in <xref ref="fig-quarter-circle-prefigure"/>, and a snapshot
        of the partially swept arc in <xref ref="fig-quarter-circle-tikz"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-video">
        <caption>
          The point <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=(\cos t,\sin t)</m> sweeps out the
          quarter circle as <m>t</m> increases from <m>0</m> to
          <m>\pi/2</m>; the accumulated arc length <m>s(t)=t</m> reaches
          <m>\pi/2\approx 1.5708</m>.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-quarter-circle" width="80%"
               source="videos/quarter_circle_arclength_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-prefigure">
        <caption>
          The quarter circle <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=(\cos t,\sin t)</m> with the
          position vector (orange) and the unit velocity vector
          <m>\mathbf{v}(t)</m> (green), which is tangent to the curve.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-quarter-circle-arclength" width="60%">
          <description>
            A quarter of the unit circle from the point one comma zero to the
            point zero comma one on a coordinate grid, with an orange position
            vector from the origin to a point on the arc and a green unit
            velocity vector tangent to the arc at that point.
          </description>
          <prefigure label="quarter-circle-arclength" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
              <definition>r(t)=(cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
              <definition>t0=0.9</definition>
              <definition>P=r(t0)</definition>
              <definition>vel=(-sin(t0), cos(t0))</definition>

              <coordinates bbox="[-0.35,-0.35,1.5,1.5]">
                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" spacing="(0.5,0.5)"/>

                <group at="arc">
                  <parametric-curve function="r" domain="(0,pi/2)" stroke="blue"
                                    thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="1.35"/>
                  <label anchor="r(0.5)" alignment="ne" offset="(12,10)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>

                <group at="endpoints">
                  <point p="(1,0)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                  <label anchor="(1,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,4)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(1,0)</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="(0,1)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                  <label anchor="(0,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,4)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(0,1)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>

                <group at="radius">
                  <vector v="P" tail="(0,0)" stroke="orange"/>
                  <point p="P" size="3.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                </group>

                <group at="velocity">
                  <vector v="vel" tail="P" stroke="green"/>
                  <label anchor="P+0.5*vel" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>\mathbf{v}(t)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
              </coordinates>

              <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure"
                            text="A quarter of the unit circle parametrized by r of t equals cosine t, sine t, for t between 0 and pi over 2, showing the position and unit velocity vectors">
                  <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                  <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                  <annotation ref="arc"
                              text="The quarter circle arc from the point 1 comma 0 to the point 0 comma 1"
                              sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="endpoints" text="The endpoints 1 comma 0 and 0 comma 1"/>
                  <annotation ref="radius" text="The position vector r of t from the origin to a point on the arc"/>
                  <annotation ref="velocity"
                              text="The velocity vector v of t, tangent to the arc, which has length one"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-tikz">
        <caption>
          Snapshot at <m>t=0.9</m>: the arc already swept out (blue) has
          length <m>s(t)=t=0.9</m>, the part not yet traced is gray, and the
          unit velocity vector <m>\mathbf{v}(t)</m> is tangent to the circle
          at the moving point.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-quarter-circle-tikz" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            A point partway along the unit quarter circle, with its position
            vector, unit velocity vector, and the arc swept so far.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              The first quadrant with a light coordinate grid. A quarter
              circle of radius one runs from the point (1,0) up to the point
              (0,1). The portion already travelled, from (1,0)
              counterclockwise to the moving point at t equals 0.9, is a
              thick blue arc ending in an arrowhead; the portion not yet
              travelled is gray. An orange position vector labelled r of t
              reaches from the origin to the moving point, drawn in red. A
              green vector labelled v of t, of length one, starts at the
              moving point, tangent to the circle and pointing in the
              direction of motion. A blue label reads s of t equals t, the
              arc length swept so far.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3.2, &gt;={Stealth[length=6pt]}]
              % light grid
              \draw[gray!25, very thin] (-0.3,-0.3) grid[step=0.5] (1.4,1.4);
              % axes
              \draw[-&gt;] (-0.35,0) -- (1.55,0) node[right] {\(x\)};
              \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.35) -- (0,1.55) node[above] {\(y\)};
              % remaining part of the quarter circle (still to be traced)
              % (0.9 rad = 51.566 degrees)
              \draw[gray!60, thick] (51.566:1) arc[start angle=51.566, end angle=90, radius=1];
              % arc already swept out, from t=0 to t=0.9
              \draw[blue, very thick, -&gt;] (1,0) arc[start angle=0, end angle=51.566, radius=1];
              % endpoints
              \fill (1,0) circle (0.55pt) node[below right=-1pt] {\((1,0)\)};
              \fill (0,1) circle (0.55pt) node[above right=-1pt] {\((0,1)\)};
              % position vector r(t)
              \draw[-&gt;, orange, thick] (0,0) -- (0.6216,0.7833)
                  node[pos=0.55, above left=-2pt] {\(\mathbf r(t)\)};
              % moving point
              \fill[red] (0.6216,0.7833) circle (0.9pt);
              % unit velocity vector v(t) = (-sin t, cos t)
              \draw[-&gt;, green!55!black, thick] (0.6216,0.7833) -- ++(-0.7833,0.6216)
                  node[pos=0.6, above right=-2pt] {\(\mathbf v(t)\)};
              % arc-length readout
              \node[blue] at (1.05,1.25) {\(s(t)=t\)};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-arc-length-3d">
    <title>Arc Length in 3D</title>

    <p>
      The formulas above generalize to motion in space with no new ideas:
      the tiny arc simply picks up a third term,
      <m>ds=\sqrt{dx^2+dy^2+dz^2}</m>. For a space curve
      <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\bigl(x(t),y(t),z(t)\bigr)</m> we therefore have
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>
        \int_A^B ds \amp
          = \int_a^b \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2
                         + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2
                         + \left(\frac{dz}{dt}\right)^2}\,dt
          = \int_a^b \left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|\,dt,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{v}(t) \amp
          = \frac{d\mathbf{r}(t)}{dt}
          = \left(\frac{dx(t)}{dt},\frac{dy(t)}{dt},\frac{dz(t)}{dt}\right),
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right| \amp
          = \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2
                + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2
                + \left(\frac{dz}{dt}\right)^2}.
      </mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      As in the plane, arc length is still the integral of speed over time.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE 2: paraglider. Statement (parts A,B,C); solution separate. -->
  <example xml:id="ex-paraglider">
    <title>Distance travelled by a paraglider</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The position of a paraglider at time <m>t</m> is
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}(t)=\left(t\cos t,\ t\sin t,\
            9\sqrt{8}-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}\,t^{3/2}\right).
        </me>
        Assuming that the paraglider begins at time <m>t=0</m>:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            How long does it take to reach the ground, that is,
            <m>z=0</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate the velocity vector at time <m>t</m>, and also
            calculate its length.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Calculate the distance travelled by the paraglider.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The paraglider reaches the ground when the
        <m>z</m>-coordinate vanishes:
        <me>9\sqrt{8}-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}\,t^{3/2}=0
            \quad\Longrightarrow\quad t^{3/2}=27
            \quad\Longrightarrow\quad t=27^{2/3}=9.</me>
        So it takes <m>9</m> units of time to land.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> We differentiate each component. The first two
        require the product rule, and for the third,
        <m>\deriv{}{t}\left(-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}t^{3/2}\right)
           =-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{2}\,t^{1/2}
           =-\frac{\sqrt{8}}{2}\,t^{1/2}=-\sqrt{2}\,t^{1/2}</m>. Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}(t)=\frac{d\mathbf{r}(t)}{dt}
          =\Bigl(\cos t-t\sin t,\ \sin t+t\cos t,\ -\sqrt{2}\,t^{1/2}\Bigr).
      </me>
      <p>
        Its length simplifies neatly: the cross terms
        <m>\pm 2t\sin t\cos t</m> cancel, the squared sines and cosines
        combine, and the last component contributes
        <m>(-\sqrt{2}\,t^{1/2})^2=2t</m>:
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right| \amp
            = \sqrt{(\cos t-t\sin t)^2+(\sin t+t\cos t)^2+2t}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \sqrt{\cos^2 t+t^2\sin^2 t-2t\sin t\cos t
                     + \sin^2 t+t^2\cos^2 t+2t\sin t\cos t+2t}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \sqrt{t^2+2t+1}=\sqrt{(t+1)^2}=t+1,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        where the last equality uses <m>t+1&gt;0</m>. So the paraglider's
        speed grows linearly: it starts at <m>1</m> and reaches <m>10</m> at
        touchdown.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> The distance travelled is the arc length of the
        path, that is, speed integrated over the <m>9</m> units of flight
        time:
      </p>
      <me>
        s=\int_0^{9}\left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|\,dt
         =\int_0^{9}(t+1)\,dt
         =\left[\frac{t^2}{2}+t\right]_0^{9}
         =\frac{81}{2}+9=49.5.
      </me>
      <p>
        The descending spiral, together with its endpoints
        <m>\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\rangle</m> and
        <m>\langle 9\cos 9,\,9\sin 9,\,0\rangle</m>, is animated in
        <xref ref="fig-paraglider-video"/> and shown in
        <xref ref="fig-paraglider-tikz"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-paraglider-video">
        <caption>
          The paraglider spirals down from
          <m>\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\rangle</m> to
          <m>\langle 9\cos 9,\,9\sin 9,\,0\rangle</m>. Because the speed is
          <m>\left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|=t+1</m>, the total distance travelled
          is <m>s=49.5</m>.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-paraglider" width="85%"
               source="videos/paraglider_arclength_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-paraglider-tikz">
        <caption>
          The paraglider's path from <m>\langle 0,0,9\sqrt{8}\rangle</m>
          down to <m>\langle 9\cos 9,\,9\sin 9,\,0\rangle</m>. The dashed
          gray curve is the path's shadow on the ground <m>z=0</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-paraglider-tikz" width="75%">
          <shortdescription>
            The paraglider's descending spiral path in three dimensions,
            from its starting point down to the ground.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
              minus 10 to 10 and z from 0 to 27. A thick blue spiral starts
              at a red point labelled start, at coordinates (0, 0, 9 root 8),
              about 25.5 units above the origin. The spiral winds outward and
              downward, its radius growing as it descends, making roughly one
              and a half turns before landing at a red point labelled ground,
              at coordinates (9 cosine 9, 9 sine 9, 0). A dashed gray spiral
              on the floor of the box shows the path's shadow on the ground.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
                width=12cm, view={115}{20},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-10, xmax=10, ymin=-10, ymax=10, zmin=0, zmax=27,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20},
              ]
              % shadow of the path on the ground z=0
              \addplot3[gray!50, dashed, thin, domain=0:9, samples=200, samples y=0]
                  ({x*cos(deg(x))}, {x*sin(deg(x))}, {0});
              % the paraglider's path
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:9, samples=200, samples y=0]
                  ({x*cos(deg(x))}, {x*sin(deg(x))}, {9*sqrt(8)-sqrt(8)/3*x^1.5});
              % start and end points
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates
                  {(0,0,25.4558) (-8.2003,3.7091,0)};
              \node[above right] at (axis cs:0,0,25.4558) {start \((0,0,9\sqrt{8})\)};
              \node[below] at (axis cs:-8.2003,3.7091,0) {ground \((9\cos 9,\ 9\sin 9,\ 0)\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
